INTRODUCTORY NOTE TO RULES 21 AND 24
The Rules above provide the basic framework for how parties can use joinder under the Federal Rules. Two sets of questions naturally follow from those Rules. First, what can a court do when the parties have not used those Rules correctly, and there are “too many” claims or parties in the case? And even if claims and parties are properly joined, what can a court do if it concludes that justice is better served by trimming down the claims and parties in the action? Rule 21 speaks to these questions, and it will play an important role in Breaking Glass Pictures v. Does 1–188, below.

Second, the rules above provide the means by which parties and court can add new claims and new parties. But what if a nonparty wants to join a case, but no current party moves to join them? This is a situation where the nonparty can move to intervene in the action and become a party. Rule 24 covers intervention, and it will be the subject of discussion in Martin v. Wilks, below.

Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 21
Misjoinder and Nonjoinder of Parties

Misjoinder of parties is not a ground for dismissing an action. On motion or on its own, the court may at any time, on just terms, add or drop a party. The court may also sever any claim against a party.

Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 24
Intervention

(a) Intervention of Right. On timely motion, the court must permit anyone to intervene who:

(1) is given an unconditional right to intervene by a federal statute; or

(2) claims an interest relating to the property or transaction that is the subject of the action, and is so situated that disposing of the action may as a practical matter impair or impede the movant’s ability
721
to protect its interest, unless existing parties adequately represent that interest.

(b) Permissive Intervention.

(1) In General. On timely motion, the court may permit anyone to intervene who:

(A) is given a conditional right to intervene by a federal statute; or

(B) has a claim or defense that shares with the main action a common question of law or fact.

(2) By a Government Officer or Agency. On timely motion, the court may permit a federal or state governmental officer or agency to intervene if a party’s claim or defense is based on:

(A) a statute or executive order administered by the officer or agency; or

(B) any regulation, order, requirement, or agreement issued or made under the statute or executive order.

(3) Delay or Prejudice. In exercising its discretion, the court must consider whether the intervention will unduly delay or prejudice the adjudication of the original parties’ rights.

(c) Notice and Pleading Required. A motion to intervene must be served on the parties * * * . The motion must state the grounds for intervention and be accompanied by a pleading that sets out the claim or defense for which intervention is sought.



•Rule 21 gives the court the power to add or remove parties or claims from a case, on motion or on its own

•Rule 24 provides conditions for granting a non-party’s motion to intervene in an action
